One parameter, every region. The model is

$\log y_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\beta, \sigma), \qquad \beta \sim \mathcal{N}(5, 1), \qquad \sigma \sim \text{half-}\mathcal{N}(1).$

```r
fit_complete <- brm(
  log(ms) ~ 1,
  data   = latency,
  family = gaussian(),
  prior  = c(prior(normal(5, 1), class = Intercept),
             prior(normal(0, 1), class = sigma)),
  chains = 4, iter = 2000, seed = 20260803
)
```

**Convergence.** All $\hat{R} \le 1.01$, bulk and tail ESS above 1,500 on both
parameters, zero divergent transitions. Nothing here is hard to sample: the posterior is
two-dimensional and unimodal.

**What it gets wrong.** The posterior predictive check is where this model fails, and it
fails visibly rather than subtly. A single $\beta$ has to sit between the fastest and
slowest regions, so the predictive distribution is too narrow in the middle and too thin
in both tails. The model is not mis-sampled; it is mis-specified, and no amount of extra
iterations repairs that.
